OBJECTION: BRANCH_RADIAL_FINE_CODES | The entropy-derived shift is along each prototype’s own radial direction, so it can systematically change prototype norms without necessarily encoding which branch or successor is relevant to the item.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: BRANCH_RADIAL_FINE_CODES | If \(h_i=0\), then \(\rho_i=0\) and behavior has no effect. If all codewords are zero, the denominator safeguard leaves their displacement direction undefined or zero, so the entropy signal cannot distinguish any prototypes.
RATIONALE: BRANCH_RADIAL_FINE_CODES | Measured successor-entropy stratification and branch coverage support 4 (E5, E10). Poincaré prototype displacement is explicit, but a matched Euclidean radial displacement is specified and no argument rules out equivalent behavior or shows curvature’s necessity (2). The computation is distinct from coarse-level changes but is close to radial-margin quantization and related successor methods in the ledger (E6), so novelty and exploration are 2. The assignment change and intended branch-specific effect are stated, though the downstream chain remains a hypothesis (3). The three modes are named, but no complete matched-control protocol or failure criterion is stated (3). The computation is affordable and mostly guarded, though the zero-codeword direction needs explicit handling (3). A bounded check of prototype norms and assignments under the stated Euclidean counterpart would help distinguish curved from radial effects.

SCORE: TRANSITION_MARGIN_FINE | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=1 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=1
SCOPE: TRANSITION_MARGIN_FINE | IN_SCOPE | Directed training transitions select successor residuals for a Poincaré-distance ranking loss that updates fine codewords and encoder; behavior-off, geometry-off, and both-on switches are specified.
EVIDENCE: TRANSITION_MARGIN_FINE | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E7 E10 E11 E12 E13 E14 E15 E16
OBJECTION: TRANSITION_MARGIN_FINE | The ranking sign appears to encourage the source’s assigned codeword to be farther from the observed successor residual than from the source residual, which may be opposite to the stated successor-compatibility motivation.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: TRANSITION_MARGIN_FINE | If the source-to-code distance is 1 and successor-to-code distance is 4, then \(s=1-4=-3\) and \(\mathrm{softplus}(s)\) is small; if the successor-to-code distance is 0 and source-to-code distance is 1, then \(s=1\) and the loss is larger. Minimizing the stated loss prefers the first, less successor-compatible configuration.
RATIONALE: TRANSITION_MARGIN_FINE | Training transitions are measured with coverage (E5, E10), supporting 4. Curved distance is used in the ranking score, but no argument shows the result cannot be reproduced by the specified matched Euclidean scoring rule (2). The evidence bundle already includes a measured tangent-successor residual mechanism and related transition-geodesic mechanisms (E6–E7), making this a close variant (novelty 1, exploration 1). A ranking-loss-to-codebook/encoder-to-recommendation route exists, but the sign objection weakens the proposed direction (3). Controls are named, but no full failure criterion or matched protocol is given (3). The computation is feasible with finite distances and a single sampled edge per item, though sign correctness should be checked (3). A bounded unit test with a hand-constructed compatible successor pair would resolve the key directional concern.

SCORE: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FINE_BASIS | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=2 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FINE_BASIS | IN_SCOPE | Training-only successor covariance defines an item-specific residual transform that is applied before hyperbolic fine-code assignment and inverted for residual reconstruction; behavior-off, geometry-off, and both-on switches are not explicitly specified in the proposal text, however, so the required three-switch testability contract is missing.
EVIDENCE: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FINE_BASIS | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E7 E10 E11 E12 E13 E14
OBJECTION: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FINE_BASIS | The covariance shrinkage and inverse-square-root formulas do not clearly specify whether \(\alpha_i\) multiplies the global-variance regularizer as written, and the behavior-off and geometry-off implementations are absent.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FINE_BASIS | For \(n_i=0\), the formula gives \(\alpha_i=0\); an item without outgoing transitions then has no stated local covariance support, and the written diagonal transform can attempt to invert a zero variance matrix unless an additional floor is defined.
RATIONALE: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FINE_BASIS | Train-only covariance is a plausible behavioral signal with measured transition support (E5, E10), but covariance-specific coverage is not measured; 4 reflects the broader behavioral evidence, not direct validation of this representation. Hyperbolic distance follows a behavior-conditioned transform, but a matched Euclidean whitening control could reproduce much of the computation and curvature necessity is unargued (2). It is computationally distinct from the listed successor-direction and separation mechanisms (E6), supporting novelty 3, though no literature search is provided. The proposed frame changes the final assignment and has a plausible recommendation path (3). Behavior-off/geometry-off switches and a failure criterion are missing in the proposal text, so falsifiability is limited (3). The diagonal transform is affordable, but zero-count covariance handling, shrinkage definition, inverse-frame decoding, and the required controls need specification (2). A bounded implementation check should verify positive diagonal floors and test the two control switches; the proposal’s novelty relative to prior work remains UNKNOWN pending a bounded literature check.